~ assignement_given

professor: Alright, listen up, everyone. I know how much you [b]love[/b] assignments, so here’s a little gift for you.  
professor: Your next assignment is due in 2 days. Yes, that means *this* week, not "whenever you feel like it."  
professor: It’s a simple task—if you actually paid attention in class, that is. Write a well-structured analysis on today's topic, using [b]real[/b] sources. And no, Wikipedia doesn't count.  
professor: Plagiarism? Oh, please, surprise me and [b]don’t[/b] copy-paste half your paper from some sketchy website. I [b]will[/b] check.  
professor: Late work? Love the enthusiasm, but no. If you have a *real* reason, tell me before the deadline, not after you've mysteriously "lost your file" five minutes before submission.  
professor: And please, do me a favor—proofread your work. If I see another essay that looks like it was written by a sleep-deprived raccoon, I might actually reconsider my career choices.  
professor: That’s it. Have fun, don’t disappoint me (again), and try not to make me regret giving you this assignment.  

=> END

~ assignment_finished

professor: Well, well, look who actually submitted their work [b]on time[/b]. I’m almost impressed.  
professor: Let’s see… Proper structure? Check. No obvious plagiarism? Check. Actual effort? Shocking, but check.  
professor: Congratulations, you’ve officially met the [b]bare minimum[/b] of academic expectations. Keep this up, and who knows? Maybe one day I’ll grade your paper without sighing first.  
do Global.player.increment_gauge(12 , "moral")
=> END  

~ assignment_incomplete  

professor: Alright, let’s take a look at—oh.  
professor: This… this isn’t finished.  
professor: Did you [b]seriously[/b] think I wouldn’t notice? Was this an attempt? A cry for help? A modern art piece symbolizing procrastination?  
professor: Whatever it is, I suggest you [b]fix it[/b], unless you’re aiming for a bold new academic strategy called [b]failing[/b].  
do Global.player.decrement_gauge(15 , "moral")

=> END  

